md on the distance computation if we first store all points contiguously.\n\nBut we cannot change tests or contract; only source files under src/. We can modify both .c and .h.\n\nLet's consider a more radical optimization: Use sorting of labels after collecting top-k? Already done.\n\nMaybe the bottleneck is parsing? But baseline includes parse. Could optimize I/O with faster scanf? Not role B.\n\nGiven constraints, we should propose one candidate transformation that is legal and likely improves speed via vectorization/loop restructuring. Let's implement:\n1. Inline euclidean_distance_no_sqrt as static inline in header.\n2. In knn(), instead of nested insertion per point, collect all distances into a dynamically allocated array (size total points) along with labels? But we need to know total count; can compute sum lengths first. Then fill arrays in one pass over groups/points, computing distance and storing. This loop is trivially vectorizable by compiler if we use simple arithmetic. Then find top-k using a selection algorithm: e.g., maintain max-heap of size k (array-based) with distances and labels. That's O(N log k). For small k, maybe similar but less branchy? Actually heap operations have branches too.\n\nAlternatively, after filling arrays, use qsort on an array of indices/structs by distance, then take first k. But sorting N elements is heavier.\n\nMaybe we can use a partial selection algorithm like quickselect to find k-th smallest, then partition and count labels among those <= threshold? That's O(N) average but complex.\n\nGiven role B specifically about SIMD/vectorization, perhaps the intended optimization is to vectorize distance computation using OpenMP simd or manual intrinsics. But ARM64 has NEON; we could use GCC vector extensions. However writing portable code with #pragma omp simd might be enough if compiler auto-vectorizes.\n\nLet's check current loop:\nfor (j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n    float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, g.points[j]);\n    ... insertion ...\n}\nThe distance computation is independent per j but the insertion creates dependency. If we separate: first compute all distances into a temporary array for each group? But then still need to merge top-k across groups.\n\nWe could restructure: For each point, instead of inserting immediately, store d and label in arrays (dist_all, labels_all) after computing all points. Then process selection. This allows the distance computation loop to be vectorized because no data dependency except storing results. The compiler can vectorize that loop if it knows alignment etc.\n\nLet's implement:\n- Compute total_points = sum of g.length.\n- Allocate arrays float *all_dists, char *all_labels (or unsigned char).\n- Loop over groups and points: all_dists[idx] = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(...); all_labels[idx] = g.label; idx++.\nThis loop is simple and vectorizable. Then find top-k smallest distances with their labels.\n\nFor selection, we can use a max-heap of size k (store largest among current best). For each i from 0 to total_points-1:\nif heap_size < k: push\nelse if all_dists[i] < heap_max: replace root and sift down.\nThis is O(N log k) with simple operations. Could be faster than insertion sort for large N? Maybe.\n\nBut we need labels associated; store pairs in arrays of structs or parallel arrays. We'll use two arrays dist_heap[k], label_heap[k]. Implement push/sift functions inline.\n\nAlternatively, since k might be small (like 5), a simple unrolled insertion could be faster than heap due to overhead. But let's try heap for generality.\n\nWe also need to handle ties? The original algorithm: if d < distances[x] or distances[x]==-1, it inserts and breaks; so equal distances are not inserted (since condition is strict less). That means among equal distances, earlier points win. Our selection must preserve that behavior exactly. If we use heap with \"less than\" comparison for replacement, we need to ensure ties don't replace existing entries. So when d == heap_max, do nothing. Also initial fill: first k points are inserted regardless (since distances[x]==-1). That matches.\n\nAfter building top-k, we have labels array of size k; then sort labels and find most frequent as before. We can reuse that code.\n\nThis transformation changes algorithm from insertion to heap selection but should be correct if tie handling is same: original inserts only when d < current distance at position x (strict). For equal distances, it does not insert because condition false (unless -1). So among equal distances, earlier points are kept. Our heap: we keep the k smallest; for ties with max, we don't replace. That preserves earliest encountered for equal values if we only replace when strictly less than current max. Good.\n\nBut careful: original insertion maintains sorted order ascending. When a new d is smaller than some existing distances, it shifts and inserts at first position where d < distances[x]. So the list contains k smallest distinct? Actually duplicates allowed; if multiple points have same distance, they can all be in top-k if there are fewer than k strictly smaller ones. The condition d < distances[x] means if equal to an existing value, it won't insert because not less. So among equal values, only the first encountered is kept (unless later a smaller one pushes it out). Our heap with strict less replacement will also keep earliest for ties at max boundary. For internal ties, they are fine.\n\nLet's implement carefully.\n\nAlso we can inline distance function and maybe use restrict? Not needed.\n\nWe'll modify knn.c only; header change to static inline might be okay but not necessary if compiler inlines due to -O3 with LTO? But no LTO likely. We can move euclidean_distance_no_sqrt into knn.c as static inline, or keep in header and mark static inline.\n\nLet's write new knn.c:\n\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <string.h> // for memcpy maybe\n\n// helper functions\nstatic void heap_push(float *dist_heap, char *label_heap, int *heap_size, float d, char label) {\n    int i = (*heap_size)++;\n    dist_heap[i] = d;\n    label_heap[i] = label;\n    while (i > 0) {\n        int parent = (i - 1) / 2;\n        if (dist_heap[parent] < dist_heap[i]) { // max-heap\n            float tmpd = dist_heap[parent]; dist_heap[parent] = dist_heap[i]; dist_heap[i] = tmpd;\n            char tmpl = label_heap[parent]; label_heap[parent] = label_heap[i]; label_heap[i] = tmpl;\n            i = parent;\n        } else break;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic void heap_replace_root(float *dist_heap, char *label_heap, int k, float d, char label) {\n    dist_heap[0] = d;\n    label_heap[0] = label;\n    int i = 0;\n    while (1) {\n        int left = 2*i + 1;\n        int right = 2*i + 2;\n        int largest = i;\n        if (left < k && dist_heap[left] > dist_heap[largest]) largest = left;\n        if (right < k && dist_heap[right] > dist_heap[largest]) largest = right;\n        if (largest != i) {\n            float tmpd = dist_heap[i]; dist_heap[i] = dist_heap[largest]; dist_heap[largest] = tmpd;\n            char tmpl = label_heap[i]; label_heap[i] = label_heap[largest]; label_heap[largest] = tmpl;\n            i = largest;\n        } else break;\n    }\n}\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    // compute total points\n    long total_points = 0;\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; gi++) total_points += groups[gi].length;\n\n    float *all_dists = (float *) malloc(sizeof(float) * total_points);\n    char *all_labels = (char *) malloc(sizeof(char) * total_points);\n\n    long idx = 0;\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; gi++) {\n        Group g = groups[gi];\n        float px = to_evaluate.x, py = to_evaluate.y;\n        // vectorizable loop\n        for (int j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n            float dx = g.points[j].x - px;\n            float dy = g.points[j].y - py;\n            all_dists[idx] = dx*dx + dy*dy;\n            all_labels[idx] = g.label;\n            idx++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // top-k selection using max-heap of size k\n    float *dist_heap = (float *) malloc(sizeof(float) * k);\n    char *label_heap = (char *) malloc(sizeof(char) * k);\n    int heap_size = 0;\n\n    for (long i = 0; i < total_points; i++) {\n        float d = all_dists[i];\n        if (heap_size < k) {\n            heap_push(dist_heap, label_heap, &heap_size, d, all_labels[i]);\n        } else if (d < dist_heap[0]) { // strictly less than current max\n            heap_replace_root(dist_heap, label_heap, k, d, all_labels[i]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(all_dists);\n    free(all_labels);\n\n    qsort(label_heap, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = label_heap[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\n        if (label_heap[i] != label_heap[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = label_heap[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            current_frequency++;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = label_heap[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(dist_heap);\n    free(label_heap);\n\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n\nBut note: original code had labels initialized to -1 and distances to -1; if k > total_points? Probably not, but we should handle. If total_points < k, then heap_size will be less than k after loop; label_heap entries beyond heap_size are uninitialized. Original would have -1 for missing slots, which when sorted might affect frequency counting (but -1 is char 255 maybe). However problem likely ensures enough points. But to be safe, we can initialize label_heap with -1 and dist_heap with INFINITY? Actually original initializes distances to -1 (which as float is