ess than max will also keep the k smallest; for equal to max, it doesn't replace, so earlier points at that distance remain. For internal duplicates, they don't affect because we only compare to max. So final set of labels corresponds to some valid top-k selection consistent with original? Need to ensure tie-breaking: Original inserts a point if there is any position where d < distances[x] or -1. This means it will insert even if equal to an earlier value, as long as later values are greater. Our heap replaces max when d < max; this may drop a larger distance and keep the new one, which matches original's insertion at first position with greater distance. The order of labels among equal distances might differ? Original shifts right all elements from x onward, so if multiple points have same distance d, they will occupy consecutive positions in order encountered (since each new one inserts before any larger values). Our heap does not maintain sorted order internally; after building we only care about multiset of labels. The frequency count depends on which labels are present, not their order. So as long as the set of k distances is correct and for ties at boundary we keep earliest encountered (which our strict less ensures), the label multiset should match original? Let's test a scenario: points with distances [1(A), 2(B), 3(C)], k=2. Original processes A: insert -> [1A, -]. B: d=2, x0 false, x1 -1 true -> insert at pos1 -> [1A,2B]. C: d=3 > both, no insert. Final labels {A,B}. Our heap: push A (heap_size 1), push B (size 2) -> heap maybe [2B,1A] max root 2; C not less than 2 so ignore. Labels {A,B} same.\n\nAnother scenario: distances [2(A), 1(B)], k=2. Original: A insert -> [2A,-]; B d=1 < 2 at x0 -> shift and insert -> [1B,2A]. Final labels {B,A}. Our heap: push A (size1), push B (size2) -> after pushes, heap might be [2A,1B] or [1B,2A] depending on implementation; but set is {A,B} same. Good.\n\nTie scenario: distances [2(A), 2(B)], k=2. Original: A insert -> [2A